@hsivonen it would be simpler, but I think less useful to developers. e.g. they would not be able to know when the on-screen images had finished loading any more. There is also the issue of not knowing when layout is stable.

It depends on the use-case for using the load event. For the use-case of determining page load time, there is already a (new) dedicated performance API for it. Another use case is test frameworks. There are probably other use-cases.

There is also the problem that once the initial batch of lazy-loaded images load, layout may change, and thereby cause more lazy-loaded images to load. So the behavior is not 100% reliable. This is an argument in favor of the load event ignoring lazy-loaded images.
